Jahshaka patch 0065 — AN ORDER-INDEPENDENT VOXEL MERGE, so a voxelisation
bucket can be a material POOL again.

SOURCE **and** MEDIA. EVERY TREE MUST RE-RUN `irisgl/scripts/build-ogre.sh`
after pulling this (the source half compiles; the media half is staged by Studio
from the SOURCE tree, so the patch loop must have run in that tree either way).
Files: `Components/Hlms/Pbs/{include,src}/Vct/OgreVctVoxelizer.{h,cpp}`,
`Samples/Media/VCT/Voxelizer.material.json`,
`Samples/Media/VCT/Voxelizer_cs.{glsl,hlsl,metal}`,
`Samples/Media/VCT/Voxelizer_piece_cs.any`, and ONE NEW media file
`Samples/Media/VCT/VoxelMerge_piece_cs.any`. The voxelisation job gains ONE uav
slot (7 instead of 6) and changes nothing else about its bindings. It shares the two VctVoxelizer
files with patches 0061, 0062 and 0064 and `Voxelizer_piece_cs.any` with 0062 —
different regions, applied after all of them.

WHAT WENT WRONG, AND WHY 0062 IS HALF RETIRED BY THIS.

`VctVoxelizer::build()` issues ONE COMPUTE DISPATCH PER BUCKET PER OCTANT, and
each dispatch is sized by the whole octant however few instances the bucket
holds. Upstream's bucket key therefore names only what a dispatch BINDS: the
compute job's variant, the material POOL's const buffer, the vertex/index
buffers, the texture-pool flag — the material SLOT rides per instance.

The voxelisation merged each dispatch's triangles into the RGBA8/RGB10A2 voxel
textures as a RUNNING MEAN (`mixAverage3/4`): load the mean written so far,
weight it by the count, add this dispatch's, store the new mean back — an 8-bit
round trip PER DISPATCH. An average of the same numbers taken in a different
order is the same value only up to that quantisation, so WHICH INSTANCES LANDED
IN WHICH DISPATCH decided the picture. And the normal's "these two surfaces face
opposite ways" test was made WHILE summing, against the sum so far, so the FIRST
triangle a thread happened to reach decided which way the voxel faced. Together
they were 6/255 on `gi.cascade_determinism`'s room between two attach orders of
one scene.

Patch 0062 bought determinism by putting the material SLOT in the bucket key —
one dispatch per MATERIAL makes the partition a property of the scene instead of
of the attach order. It worked, and it cost: on a scene whose 8,001 primitives
each own a material (which is what the editor gives every primitive) the
outermost cascade's rebuild went **86 -> 325 ms**, 3.8x, because thousands of
whole-volume dispatches at ~28 us each is a quarter of a second of nothing.
Scenes that SHARE materials paid nothing.

THE FIX — ACCUMULATE, THEN RESOLVE, IN ONE DISPATCH.

1. A voxelisation dispatch adds its triangles to PER-VOXEL INTEGER SUMS in a new
   accumulator (`mMergeAccumTex`, PFG_R32_UINT, sixteen texels per voxel
   interleaved in Z: albedo sum, raw normal sum, emissive sum, FOLDED normal sum,
   four channels each), on the fixed-point grid `VoxelMerge_piece_cs.any` defines
   (12 fractional bits, one contribution clamped to [0, 16], so 65,535
   contributions cannot overflow a uint32 and the snap is 6 % of one step of the
   8-bit texture the mean finally lands in). Integer addition is exact and
   associative: the partition stops mattering. Upstream's own `voxelAccumVal`
   stays the triangle counter and the divisor, read and written where upstream
   read and wrote it.

2. It then writes the mean of the TOTAL the accumulator now holds into the three
   voxel textures. Whichever dispatch is last at a voxel therefore leaves the
   complete answer and an earlier one's partial answer is always overwritten by
   it — the value depends on the sums, not on who wrote them — so no separate
   resolve pass is needed. A dispatch also stops visiting voxels it contributed
   nothing to (upstream's condition was `accumTris + origAccumTris > 0`, so a
   dispatch re-rounded every voxel an earlier one had filled), which is most of
   them.

3. The "opposite ways" decision moves into that resolve, and needs no threshold,
   because the two normal sums GIVE BACK THE TWO GROUPS. Each normal also goes
   into a FOLDED sum, folded into one half-space by the sign of its largest
   component, so ANTIPODAL NORMALS ALWAYS FOLD TOGETHER. Write the folded sum as
   U - F and the raw one as U + F, where U is the sum of the normals the fold
   left alone and F the sum, in their ORIGINAL directions, of the ones it turned
   round: then U = (raw + folded)/2 and F = (raw - folded)/2, both exact. The
   voxel's normal is the LARGER of the two — the majority group's mean, which is
   what upstream stored after it dropped the opposing triangles and divided by
   the whole count — and it is double-sided when the OTHER group is not empty,
   which is what upstream's exclusion meant. Both emptiness tests are INTEGER
   comparisons of the two sums, so a minority of one triangle in sixty thousand
   still counts, and a voxel whose normals never crossed the fold's seam cannot
   raise the flag.
   Taking the LARGER and not U is load-bearing: a voxel whose normals all lie in
   the half-space the fold turns round (a wall facing -X) has U = 0, and storing
   that leaves it black — measured at four `gi.leak_room` assertions.
   The one place this is not upstream's answer: it dropped a triangle at more
   than 120 degrees from the RUNNING sum while the fold separates at 90, so a
   voxel whose normals span 90 to 120 degrees is two groups here and one there.
   Measured on gi.field_follows' sub-voxel walls that is worth under 3 % of the
   far bounce (0.0216 against the pin's 0.0210), in the safe direction.

4. `VoxelizerBucket::materialSlotIdx` is therefore RETIRED — patch 0062's source
   half, and only that half. Its media half (the `max(x, x)` typo on the
   double-sided flag) is superseded by the resolve computing the flag rather than
   merging it. The bucket key keeps 0062's pointer-free ordering (variant,
   material POOL, texture-pool flag) because a stable dispatch order is still the
   right thing for a renderer to have and now costs nothing.

R32_UINT AND NOT RGBA32_UINT, WHICH WOULD BE A QUARTER OF THE TEXELS AND A
QUARTER OF THE ADDRESSING: `clearVoxels` clears the accumulator every build
through `ComputeTools::clearUavUint`, and clearing a PFG_RGBA32_UINT 3D uav that
way HANGS THE GPU on this driver — `VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST` with `NVRM: Xid 109
CTX SWITCH TIMEOUT`, 8 runs out of 8 on `samples.cleanstart.Showroom` and
`Showroom_2` (NVIDIA 595.84, RTX 4080 SUPER), and 0 out of 4 with that one call
removed while the same texture was still created, still bound and still written
by the same shader. It is the CLEAR and not the size, the binding, the shader
body or the bucket key: each of those was reversed on its own and the device was
still lost. R32_UINT clears without complaint, 4/4. Recorded as an upstream /
driver finding; ComputeTools' clear job is generic and the fault is not visible
in its shader, so it is dodged here rather than fixed.

WHAT IT COSTS. The accumulator is 64 bytes per voxel — 16.8 MB at 64^3, 134 MB
at 128^3 — and it is TRANSIENT: like `mAccumValVox` it is scheduled back
OnStorage the moment `build()` returns, and one volume is voxelised at a time.
Per touched voxel a dispatch now reads and writes sixteen scalar texels beside
the three it always wrote.

MEASURED (lane VOXMERGE-1, spikes/voxmerge-1/, RTX 4080 SUPER, GPU clocks
locked, the 8,026-instance lattice at Photon High, GPU timestamps, two runs per
arm, mean / max GPU ms per rebuild):

    cascade      c0            c1            c2            c3 (outermost)
    0001-0064    13.7 / 20.4   34.3 / 90.1   73.9 / 175.5  324.8 / 333.6
                 14.3 / 22.7   29.8 / 73.4   68.5 / 164.0  329.1 / 331.1
    0001-0065    13.9 / 17.0   18.1 / 32.1   18.2 / 28.7    84.1 /  84.2
                 13.6 / 16.7   17.3 / 29.7   18.3 / 28.8    84.3 /  84.9

The outermost cascade is BELOW the pre-0062 number (E2 measured 86.8 / 88.9
before 0062 landed) — 3.9x. Showroom 2, whose materials are shared, pays the
accumulator's traffic on its near cascades and is well repaid on its far one:
c0 4.21 -> 4.71, c1 4.31 -> 4.79, c2 3.77 -> 3.80, c3 7.84 -> 4.58.

`gi.cascade_determinism` case 3 goes **6.00/255 -> 0.00/255** — exact, not merely
inside the 8-bit floor — and its new case 4 (200 objects, 200 materials, two
attach orders) reads 0.00/255 too. `--engine-selftest` is **byte-identical**
(`ead9a2ce6ddcc3badb7e1c6226db7ebd38a19b36a2359b99ea84e1d42c8b215d`), and so is
the single-volume (chain OFF) picture of the same scene.

UPSTREAM. Two findings for SPECS/OGRE_UPSTREAM_ISSUES.md: the order-dependent
voxel merge itself (upstream already knows the class —
`OGRE_FORCE_VCT_VOXELIZER_DETERMINISTIC` orders `MeshPtr`s by name for the same
reason — and does not cover the voxel merge or the normal clustering), and the
`ComputeTools` RGBA32_UINT 3D clear that loses the device.

VERIFICATION. `git apply --check` on a tree carrying 0001-0064 and
`git apply --reverse --check` after applying; `build-ogre.sh` re-run in the lane
worktree. Suites: `gi.cascade_determinism` (which gains case 4 — 200 objects with
200 materials in two attach orders), `gi.cascades` case 14 (the dispatch count is
a handful, not one per material), every other `gi.*`, `scene.reopen_fidelity`,
`samples.cleanstart.*`, `test_engine` and its ASan twin.

